WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.165 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The school garden tank is filling.

00:00:02.165 --> 00:00:06.856 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rising line looks promising, but can we promise what the
tank will hold tomorrow?

00:00:06.856 --> 00:00:10.676 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture two garden tanks beside the school vegetable beds.

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:14.059 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Rain adds water and watering the plants removes it.

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:17.587 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A class records the amount on the same four school days.

00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:22.909 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A line graph can help us follow change, but only when we
know what its axes and units mean.

00:00:22.909 --> 00:00:27.930 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to interpret line graphs and explain
what their evidence supports.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:32.904 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our goal is to interpret observations over time and make
careful comparisons.

00:00:32.904 --> 00:00:38.850 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to read a particular day, calculate a change between
days, and explain which conclusions are supported.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:42.276 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep those three different jobs separate as we build the
graph.

00:00:42.276 --> 00:00:45.482 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can read the time axis and the labelled scale.

00:00:45.482 --> 00:00:49.054 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can compare changes over equal time intervals.

00:00:49.054 --> 00:00:52.817 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can separate observations from uncertain predictions.

00:00:52.817 --> 00:00:56.587 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Remember reading a table by matching a row label with a
quantity.

00:00:56.587 --> 00:00:59.796 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will turn those table observations into points.

00:00:59.796 --> 00:01:03.769 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday belong in time
order.

00:01:03.769 --> 00:01:09.020 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Equal spaces along the time axis will represent equal
one-day intervals.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.261 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A line graph shows observations in time order.

00:01:12.261 --> 00:01:18.012 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The horizontal axis shows time and the vertical axis shows
the measured quantity and unit.

00:01:18.012 --> 00:01:21.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Equal spaces must mean equal time intervals.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.889 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Read the scale before comparing heights.

00:01:23.889 --> 00:01:29.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rising segment means an increase, a falling one a decrease
and a flat one no change.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:35.400 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The line connects observations; it does not prove every
value between them or any future value.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.004 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start with the horizontal time axis.

00:01:38.004 --> 00:01:41.159 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Add the days in order and leave equal spacing.

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:47.829 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertical axis measures litres, starting at zero and
increasing by ten litres at each marked interval.

00:01:47.829 --> 00:01:52.700 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Plot Monday at ten, Tuesday at twenty, Wednesday at twenty
and Thursday at forty.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:58.100 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Join consecutive observations so the segments show changes
between recorded days.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.895 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A tank holds 10, 20, 20 and 40 litres on Monday, Tuesday,
Wednesday and Thursday.

00:02:03.895 --> 00:02:06.802 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which equal daily interval rose most?

00:02:06.802 --> 00:02:11.858 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before deciding which increase is largest, look at each pair
of neighbouring days.

00:02:11.858 --> 00:02:18.448 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the change from Monday to Tuesday, then from Tuesday
to Wednesday, and finally from Wednesday to Thursday.

00:02:18.448 --> 00:02:20.789 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep your three changes in mind.

00:02:20.789 --> 00:02:24.260 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which interval do you think has the greatest increase?

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.951 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Changes are +10 L, 0 L and +20 L.

00:02:32.951 --> 00:02:35.437 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Wednesday to Thursday rose most.

00:02:35.437 --> 00:02:39.408 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The highest point and the largest rise answer different
questions.

00:02:39.408 --> 00:02:44.552 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tuesday’s twenty litres minus Monday’s ten gives an increase
of ten litres.

00:02:44.552 --> 00:02:48.655 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two middle readings are equal, so that interval has no
change.

00:02:48.655 --> 00:02:53.519 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Thursday’s forty minus Wednesday’s twenty gives an increase
of twenty litres.

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:55.546 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That last rise is greatest.

00:02:55.546 --> 00:02:59.485 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The highest reading and the largest change are different
questions.

00:02:59.485 --> 00:03:02.183 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tank A: 10,20,20,40 L.

00:03:02.183 --> 00:03:05.996 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tank B: 20,20,30,30 L on the same days.

00:03:05.996 --> 00:03:08.076 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which is fuller on Thursday?

00:03:08.076 --> 00:03:09.878 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can we promise Friday?

00:03:09.878 --> 00:03:14.532 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tank B has twenty, twenty, thirty and thirty litres on the
same days.

00:03:14.532 --> 00:03:17.702 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It uses the same time and quantity scales.

00:03:17.702 --> 00:03:23.034 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before we reveal a comparison, find Thursday on both lines
and read the two values.

00:03:23.034 --> 00:03:26.727 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which tank is fuller, and what subtraction will tell you the
difference?

00:03:31.727 --> 00:03:36.189 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A has 40 L and B has 30 L on Thursday, a difference of 10 L.

00:03:36.189 --> 00:03:40.484 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need new observations for Friday: rain and water use may
change.

00:03:40.484 --> 00:03:46.113 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If a vertical axis starts above zero, a small change can
look dramatic; read the labels.

00:03:46.113 --> 00:03:51.050 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The solid circle line represents A, while the dashed square
line represents B.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:54.275 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On Thursday, A has forty litres and B has thirty.

00:03:54.275 --> 00:03:56.369 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The difference is ten litres.

00:03:56.369 --> 00:03:58.709 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
On Monday, B was fuller instead.

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:06.382 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Comparing at the same time is important: a Monday point on
one line and a Thursday point on the other answer a
different question.

00:04:06.382 --> 00:04:11.833 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
“A rising line must keep rising.” It records what happened
during the shown times.

00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:15.440 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A forecast is uncertain and needs extra evidence.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.840 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The line between observations helps us see change, but it
does not record every moment.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.072 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A tank might be used during the day, then filled again.

00:04:24.072 --> 00:04:26.886 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Friday is outside our observations.

00:04:26.886 --> 00:04:31.445 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can suggest what might happen, but the graph alone cannot
promise a reading.

00:04:31.445 --> 00:04:36.069 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need new observations and information about rain or water
use.

00:04:36.069 --> 00:04:40.125 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Pause the player and try the investigation in the written
lesson.

00:04:40.125 --> 00:04:44.728 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make a prediction, collect or change the data, then explain
what you notice.

00:04:44.728 --> 00:04:48.349 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at two graphs of a forty-to-fifty-litre change.

00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:52.685 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One vertical scale begins at zero and the other begins at
forty.

00:04:52.685 --> 00:04:56.580 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The second makes the rise look much larger on the page.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.672 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The observations still differ by ten litres.

00:04:59.672 --> 00:05:04.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Always read the scale labels before deciding how large a
change really is.

00:05:04.649 --> 00:05:08.979 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Describe one interval using its two days and change in
litres.

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:11.433 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Write one claim the graph cannot prove.

00:05:11.433 --> 00:05:14.824 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In the explorer, change Tank A’s Thursday reading.

00:05:14.824 --> 00:05:19.018 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict which interval now has the biggest rise, then check
the values.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:22.491 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If two intervals rise by the same amount, say they tie.

00:05:22.491 --> 00:05:26.773 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A flat interval has zero change, and a falling interval has
a decrease.

00:05:26.773 --> 00:05:30.060 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the day names and litres in your explanation.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.303 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What can your line graph tell you confidently, and what does
it leave uncertain?

00:05:34.303 --> 00:05:38.748 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Share one claim using a day and a reading, then one claim
about a change.

00:05:38.748 --> 00:05:42.828 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Finish by naming one question that would need more
observations.

00:05:42.828 --> 00:05:47.022 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Clear axes and careful comparisons help the evidence speak.
